    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d218">

                <pb facs="0326" n="214" sameAs="frus:frus1919Russia/pg214"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.51/658: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Reinsch</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content" calendar="gregorian"
                            notAfter="1919-08-29T15:52:00-04:00"
                            notBefore="1919-08-27T18:00:00+04:53"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >undated</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received August 29, 1919, 3:52
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>From [Harris at] Omsk.</p>
                <p>“370, August 27, 6 p.m. Today Eliot,<anchor n="14" xml:id="d218-f1"/>
                    Martel<anchor n="15" xml:id="d218-f2"
                    /> and myself advised Kolchak
                    immediately remove gold reserve amounting (only two?) hundred million roubles to
                    Vladivostok. All foreign military experts here agree this should be done as
                    precaution. Kolchak refused, stating he
                    felt able to evacuate gold later if necessary. Harris.[”]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Reinsch</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d218-f1" n="14" xml:id="d218-f1-content">
                <p>Sir Charles Eliot, British High
                    Commissioner in Siberia.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d218-f2" n="15" xml:id="d218-f2-content">
                <p>Damien J. A. C., Count de Martel,
                    French High Commissioner <hi rendition="#italic">ad interim</hi> in Siberia.</p>
            </note>
